\section{Errors}{


The code before the first \code{await()} runs right away, when the async
function is called. An error there is thrown directly by the call,
like in a regular function. Once the function has reached an
\code{await()}, the rest runs later from the event loop and errors reject
the returned promise instead.

To have all errors reject the promise, start the function with
\code{await(NULL)}. Everything after it runs from the event loop:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{fn <- async(function() \{
  await(NULL)
  stop("Rejects the promise")
\})
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Values can change across an \code{await()}}{


The code after an \code{await()} runs later in time. Values that the
function has not read yet may have changed by then. This applies to
arguments too, because R evaluates them lazily: an argument that is
first used after an \code{await()} is evaluated at that later time.

To avoid surprises, capture the values you need before the first
\code{await()}, with \code{x <- force(x)} for an argument or
\code{y <- force(some_val)} for any other value:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{fn <- async(function(x) \{
  x <- force(x)
  y <- force(some_val)
  await(async_sleep(0.1))
  list(x, y)
\})
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

